# `findFirst` Function

```javascript
function findFirst(arr, predicate) {
  for (let i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
    if (predicate(arr[i], i, arr)) {
      return arr[i];
    }
  }
  return null;
}
```

## Usage Examples

```javascript
const numbers = [3, 8, 12, 5, 20];

findFirst(numbers, n => n > 10);   // 12
findFirst(numbers, n => n > 100); // null

const users = [
  { name: "Alice", age: 30 },
  { name: "Bob", age: 25 }
];

findFirst(users, u => u.name === "Bob"); // { name: "Bob", age: 25 }
```

## Notes

- The predicate receives `(element, index, array)`, matching the native `Array.prototype.find` convention.
- Returns `null` when no match is found (unlike the native `.find()`, which returns `undefined`).
- If you're okay using built-ins, this is equivalent to:

```javascript
const findFirst = (arr, predicate) => arr.find(predicate) ?? null;
```

The `?? null` converts `undefined` to `null` (but preserves falsy matches like `0` or `""`).

