    }
  }
  return null;
}
```

**Usage examples:**

```javascript
const nums = [3, 8, 12, 5];

findFirst(nums, n => n > 10);   // 12
findFirst(nums, n => n < 0);    // null
findFirst(nums, (n, i) => i === 2); // 12 (matches by index)
```

**Notes:**
- Iterates in order and returns the **first** match, stopping early (efficient for large arrays).
- Returns `null` when nothing matches.
- The predicate receives `(element, index, array)` — same signature as the built-in `Array.prototype.find`, except this returns `null` instead of `undefined` when no match is found.

If you prefer, you could also implement it as a one-liner using the built-in method:

```javascript
